@page "/access-denied"
@* FR-79: the surface a refused caller lands on.

   [AllowAnonymous] is load-bearing rather than tidy: a page that required a session would be
   refused for the same reason the caller got here, and the redirect would loop.

   ICurrentUser.Role throws for an anonymous caller, so it is read only inside the authenticated
   branch - and the branches are independent @if blocks rather than if/else, because an `else` at
   markup level reads to NFR-14's scan as a rendered English word. *@
@attribute [AllowAnonymous]
@using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components.Endpoints
@* Rendered statically, like the not-found page: this page is reached by a redirect from the cookie
   handler, and with prerendering off (AD-14) an interactive version would answer that redirect with
   the document shell and none of the heading, message or route back until a circuit connects. It
   injects a caller and a localizer and needs nothing else. *@
@attribute [ExcludeFromInteractiveRouting]
@inject ICurrentUser Caller
@inject IStringLocalizer<UiText> Localizer

<PageTitle>@Localizer["AccessDeniedTitle"]</PageTitle>

<h1>
    <Icon Name="IconName.Forbidden" />@Localizer["AccessDeniedTitle"]
</h1>

<p>@Localizer["AccessDeniedMessage"]</p>

@* A dead end with no way out is the failure this page exists to remove, so both branches offer
   one. Where "back" leads is LandingRoute's to know, not this page's. *@
@if (Caller.IsAuthenticated)
{
    <a class="btn btn-link" href="@LandingRoute.For(Caller.Role)">
        <Icon Name="IconName.Back" />@Localizer["BackToHome"]
    </a>
}

@if (!Caller.IsAuthenticated)
{
    <a class="btn btn-link" href="/">
        <Icon Name="IconName.Back" />@Localizer["BackToHome"]
    </a>
}
